.TH CALCYX 1 "2026" "calcyx" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
calcyx \- engineer's calculator (CLI / TUI / GUI)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B calcyx
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI [ FILE ...]
.br
.B calcyx-gui
.RI [ FILE ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B calcyx
is a multi-platform engineer's calculator.  Expressions may be entered
one per line; the result of a previous line is available as a value to
later lines.  The program runs as a TUI (default in a terminal), as a
CLI batch evaluator (with
.BR \-e ,
.BR \-o ,
.BR \-b ,
or via a pipe), or as an FLTK GUI when invoked as
.BR calcyx-gui .
.PP
A single binary,
.BR calcyx ,
provides both the CLI and the TUI; the mode is selected automatically
from the command-line arguments and from whether standard input is a
terminal.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-e " EXPR"
Evaluate
.I EXPR
directly (CLI mode).  May be repeated.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " " \fIFORMAT\fR
Output format for CLI evaluation:
.B result
(default) prints only the result;
.B both
prints "expr = result".
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-batch
Force CLI batch evaluation of positional files or standard input.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-repl
Start the legacy line-based REPL.
.TP
.B \-\-print\-config
Print the resolved configuration in canonical form on stdout.
.TP
.B \-\-check\-config
Syntax-check the configuration file; exit 1 if warnings are produced.
.TP
.B \-\-init\-config
Generate a default configuration file if one does not yet exist.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Use
.I PATH
instead of the platform-default configuration file for
.BR \-\-print\-config ,
.BR \-\-check\-config ,
and
.BR \-\-init\-config .
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a short help message and exit.
.SH MODES
The mode is selected as follows:
.IP \(bu 2
If
.BR \-e ,
.BR \-o ,
.BR \-b ,
or
.B \-r
is given, CLI mode is used.
.IP \(bu 2
If standard input is not a terminal (pipe or redirection), CLI mode is used.
.IP \(bu 2
Otherwise (interactive terminal, optionally with a positional file),
TUI mode is used and any positional file is loaded.
.SH COMMENTS
A semicolon (\(oq;\(cq) introduces a line comment.  Everything after
the first semicolon outside of a string or character literal is ignored.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/calcyx/calcyx.conf
User configuration (Linux).  See
.B \-\-print\-config
for the canonical form.
.TP
.I ~/.config/calcyx/calcyx.conf.override
Optional override file whose entries take precedence over the user
configuration; intended for users who prefer textual configuration.
.TP
.I /usr/share/calcyx/samples/
Bundled sample files (Linux package layout).
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Start the TUI:
.PP
.RS 4
.B calcyx
.RE
.PP
Open a file in the TUI:
.PP
.RS 4
.B calcyx file.txt
.RE
.PP
Evaluate a single expression from the shell:
.PP
.RS 4
.B calcyx -e '1+1' -e 'x=42'
.RE
.PP
Batch-evaluate a file:
.PP
.RS 4
.B calcyx --batch file.txt
.RE
.PP
Pipe an expression in:
.PP
.RS 4
.B echo 'hex(255)' | calcyx
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Normal exit.
.TP
.B 1
Evaluation error.
.TP
.B 2
Argument or file-handling error.
.SH SEE ALSO
The bundled samples in
.I /usr/share/calcyx/samples/
illustrate the expression language.
